<SiteLayout title={cas.title} activeSection="manual" activeSubject="air-to-ground-operations" compactSubjectNav={true}
 breadcrumbs={[{label:'Manual',href:sitePath('manual/')},{label:'Air-to-Ground Operations',href:sitePath('manual/air-to-ground-operations/')},{label:cas.title}]}>
 <section class="status-panel" data-module-id={cas.id} data-module-revision={cas.revision}>
  <p>Module revision {cas.revision} · How an attack close to friendly troops is controlled, briefed and cleared, from the cockpit's side. For fixed- and rotary-wing aircrew of every service, from the joint close air support publication.</p>
 </section>
 <nav class="contents" aria-labelledby="contents-title"><h2 id="contents-title">In this module</h2>
  <div class="contents-groups">
   <div><h3>The core sequence</h3><ol>{core.map(([id,label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}</ol></div>
   <div><h3>Reference</h3><ol>{reference.map(([id,label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}</ol></div>
  </div>
 </nav>

 <p class="lede">Close air support is a conversation. A controller on the ground and an aircrew overhead, who may never have worked together and may come from different services, have a few minutes to agree on one target, the friendly troops near it and how the attack will be flown. They do it with a small set of standard formats and calls. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-1, V-1')}</a> Learn those, in order, and the rest of this module is detail.</p>
 <p>By the end you should be able to:</p>
 <ol class="outcomes">
  <li>say who may control a CAS attack and what a <Term>FAC(A)</Term> can take over;</li>
  <li>tell the type of control from the method of attack;</li>
  <li>give a <Term>CAS check-in</Term> and copy a <Term>9-line</Term>, with each line's units;</li>
  <li>give the <Term>mandatory readback</Term>;</li>
  <li>explain <Term>correlation</Term> and the laser and infrared calls;</li>
  <li>use the clearance calls and know who can abort;</li>
  <li>explain <Term>danger close</Term> and why attack headings run parallel to friendly lines;</li>
  <li>say how helicopter CAS and its <Term>5-line</Term> differ, and why Army attack helicopters are a special case.</li>
 </ol>
 <p class="note">This is a reference, not a qualification: only a certified and qualified <Term>JTAC</Term> or <Term>FAC(A)</Term> may control an attack. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-2')}</a> Flying the delivery itself is in <a href={wd}>Weapons Delivery</a>, and radio technique in <a href={radio}>Radio Procedures</a>. The worked example is invented in the publication's format. The publication quoted is the 2014 edition; theater special instructions (SPINS), your unit's procedures and the current edition govern.</p>

 <h2 id="example">One attack, start to finish</h2>
 <p>Here is a whole Type 1 attack by a two-ship, as it might sound on the radio. Gunsmoke 22 is the JTAC; Viper 41 leads the flight. Every call follows a format from the publication, and each step links to the section that explains it. The call signs, places and numbers are invented. Figure 1 below plays it out on a map, and Figure 2 draws the brief line by line.</p>
 <ol class="exchange script">
  <li><span class="who">JTAC</span> <span class="said">"Viper 41, Gunsmoke 22, maintain Hammer, block 15 to 17." <a class="step" href="#checkin">Routing</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">JTAC</span> <span class="said">"Viper 41, send your check-in."</span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Viper 41</span> <span class="said">"Gunsmoke 22, Viper 41, mission 4123, 2 by A-10C, 10 miles east of Hammer, block 15 to 17, four Mk 82, playtime 40 minutes, targeting pods." <a class="step" href="#checkin">Check-in</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">JTAC</span> <span class="said">A short situation update: the threat, and "all friendlies are south of the 56 northing". <a class="step" href="#checkin">Situation update</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">JTAC</span> <span class="said">"Viper 41, {EXAMPLE.gamePlan}; advise when ready for 9-line." <a class="step" href="#control">Game plan</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Viper 41</span> <span class="said">"Viper 41, ready to copy."</span></li>
  <li><span class="who">JTAC</span> <span class="said">"{say('ip')}, {say('heading')}, {say('distance')}." <br/>"{say('elevation')}, {say('description')}, {say('location')}." <br/>"{say('mark')}, {say('friendlies')}, {say('egress')}." <br/>"{say('remarks')}." <a class="step" href="#nineline">9-line</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Viper 41</span> <span class="said">"{readback()}." <a class="step" href="#readback">Readback</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">JTAC</span> <span class="said">"Viper 41, readback correct."</span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Viper 41</span> <span class="said">"Viper 41, CONTACT the mark." <a class="step" href="#correlation">Correlation</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">JTAC</span> <span class="said">"Viper 41, from the mark, east 50, the western vehicle is your target."</span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Viper 41</span> <span class="said">"Viper 41, TALLY."</span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Viper 41</span> <span class="said">"Viper 41, IN heading 270." <a class="step" href="#attack">Attack</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">JTAC</span> <span class="said">"Viper 41, CLEARED HOT." <a class="step" href="#clearance">Clearance</a></span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Viper 41</span> <span class="said">"Viper 41, off, two away."</span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Viper 42</span> <span class="said">Thirty seconds behind, calls TALLY, then makes its own IN call and gets its own clearance: here each Type 1 attack is cleared on its own, though a controller can also clear the whole flight at once.</span></li>
  <li><span class="who">JTAC</span> <span class="said">Battle damage assessment for the flight as it egresses north to Anvil. <a class="step" href="#attack">Assessment</a></span></li>
 </ol>
 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="play" aria-labelledby="play-title">
  <h3 id="play-title" class="fig-title">Figure 1 — The attack, played out</h3>
  <p>Step through the exchange above and watch where the flight is at each call. Switch to Type 3 to see how the attack changes when one clearance covers the whole engagement.</p>
  <div class="buttons chips" data-types role="group" aria-label="Type of control"></div>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 1, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 640 210" role="img" aria-label="Plan view of the example attack: the flight checks in east of IP Hammer, holds there for the brief, runs in heading 270 to the target, and egresses north to Anvil"></svg></div>
  <div class="buttons stepper"><button type="button" data-back>Back</button><button type="button" data-next>Next</button><span data-count role="status" aria-live="polite"></span></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. The flight checks in 10 NM east of IP Hammer, holds at Hammer for the game plan, the 9-line and the readback, correlates on the smoke, then runs in heading 270. Under Type 1 each aircraft calls IN and is CLEARED HOT on its own; under Type 3 the JTAC says CLEARED TO ENGAGE once, the lead calls COMMENCING ENGAGEMENT and ENGAGEMENT COMPLETE, and no pass needs its own clearance. The flight egresses north to Anvil.</p>
  <div class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">The opening example, invented in JP 3-09.3's format and drawn to Figure 2's scale. The calls follow the execution template <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-14 to V-50')}</a>; the Type 3 calls are from <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-46')}</a>. The aircraft positions are illustrative, with the second aircraft 30 seconds behind the lead.</p>
 </section>

 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> every attack follows the same outline: check in, agree the game plan, brief, read back, correlate, attack. This example is Type 1 bomb on target, so the aircrew must see the target and each pass gets its own IN and CLEARED HOT; under <a href="#control">Type 3</a> one CLEARED TO ENGAGE covers several attacks, and in a bomb-on-coordinate attack the aircrew never has to see the target. The sections below take it one step at a time.</p>

 <h3 id="flow">The sequence</h3>
 <p>The publication's execution template, so that aircrew and controllers can expect the same steps every time: <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-14 to V-50')}</a></p>
 <ol class="steps">
  <li><a href="#checkin">Routing and safety of flight</a>: at least a "maintain" instruction, then other aircraft, threats, weather and terrain. "Razor 53, maintain Chevy-Dodge 14-15". <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-14 to V-15')}</a></li>
  <li><a href="#checkin">Check-in</a>, when the controller asks for it.</li>
  <li><a href="#checkin">Situation update</a>, cut to what this aircraft needs.</li>
  <li><a href="#control">Game plan</a>: type of control and method of attack.</li>
  <li><a href="#nineline">CAS brief</a>: the <Term>9-line</Term>, then remarks and restrictions.</li>
  <li><a href="#readback">Readbacks</a>.</li>
  <li><a href="#correlation">Correlation</a>: confirming the aircrew has the right target or mark.</li>
  <li><a href="#attack">Attack</a>: <Term>IN</Term> call, clearance, release.</li>
  <li><a href="#attack">Assess</a> the effects, and reattack or rebrief.</li>
  <li><a href="#attack">Battle damage assessment</a>.</li>
  <li>Routing and safety of flight again on egress: a point and an altitude block clear of other aircraft and fires. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-50')}</a></li>
 </ol>

 <h2 id="what">What close air support is</h2>
 <p><Term>CAS</Term> is air action by fixed- and rotary-wing aircraft against hostile targets in close proximity to friendly forces, which requires detailed integration of each air mission with the fire and movement of those forces. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-1')}</a> "Close" is not a distance. What makes an attack CAS is the need for that integration, because of proximity, fires or movement. CAS may destroy, disrupt, suppress, fix, harass, neutralize or delay enemy forces. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-2')}</a></p>
 <p>The ground commander owns the fires. The lowest-level ground commander employs the CAS assets unless a higher commander keeps that authority, and decides the target priority, effects and timing. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-3')}</a> Aircrews keep the primary responsibility for weaponeering recommendations and their own tactics; the <Term>JTAC</Term> or <Term>FAC(A)</Term> concentrates on the effect on the target. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-4')}</a></p>
 <details class="more"><summary>What makes CAS work well</summary>
  <p>Well-trained people, effective planning and integration, effective command and control, air superiority (especially suppression of enemy air defenses), target marking or acquisition, streamlined and flexible procedures, and the right ordnance. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-6')}</a></p>
 </details>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> CAS is defined by integration with nearby troops, not by distance, and the ground commander decides what is attacked.</p>

 <h2 id="who">Who controls the attack</h2>
 <p><Term>Terminal attack control</Term> is the authority to control the maneuver of attacking aircraft and to grant weapons release clearance. A certified and qualified <Term>JTAC</Term> or <Term>FAC(A)</Term> is recognized across the Department of Defense as authorized to do it, whichever service trained them. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-2')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Who does what in a CAS attack" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>Who does what</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Who</th><th scope="col">What they do</th><th scope="col">Can clear an attack?</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row"><Term>JTAC</Term></th><td>A qualified (certified) service member who, from a forward position, directs aircraft engaged in CAS. <a href="#source-jp">{j('GL-11')}</a> The supported commander delegates weapons release authority to the JTAC for specific engagements. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-40')}</a></td><td>Yes</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row"><Term>FAC(A)</Term></th><td>A specifically trained and qualified aviator, normally an airborne extension of the <Term>TACP</Term>, controlling CAS from the air. <a href="#source-jp">{j('GL-10')}</a> Says "FAC(A) capable" or "qualified" at check-in; can control under all three types, relay calls, reconnoiter and recommend weapons, call for artillery, deconflict aircraft and fires, mark, talk aircraft on and pass battle damage assessment. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-55 to V-57')}</a></td><td>Yes</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row"><Term>JFO</Term></th><td>Requests, adjusts and controls surface fires and provides targeting information in support of Type 2 and 3 control by a JTAC or FAC(A). <a href="#source-jp">{j('GL-11')}</a></td><td>No</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Anyone doing <Term>terminal guidance operations</Term></th><td>Lasing a target or generating a coordinate, for example. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-2')}</a></td><td>No, unless also a qualified JTAC or FAC(A)</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Air control agency</th><td>Usually briefs the aircraft from the request before it reaches the controller: the Air Force's <Term>ASOC</Term>, the Marine Corps' <Term>DASC</Term>, a Marine or Navy tactical air control center, or a <Term>TAC(A)</Term>. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-3')}</a> <a href="#source-jp">{j('GL-8 to GL-9, GL-13')}</a></td><td>No</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p><strong>Splitting the job.</strong> A JTAC working with a FAC(A) can hand over any of four duties, named by brevity words each must echo back. Whoever holds final release authority must be stated clearly. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-57 to V-58')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="JTAC and FAC(A) duties" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>The four duties a JTAC can give a FAC(A) <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-57 to V-58')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Duty</th><th scope="col">The FAC(A)…</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">BRIEF</th><td>passes area updates, CAS briefs and battle damage assessment, and copies check-ins</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">STACK</th><td>deconflicts the CAS aircraft from each other and from surface fires, including holding, ingress and egress</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">MARK</th><td>marks targets (a talk-on counts); marking on its own, it still calls <Term>IN</Term> so the JTAC can abort it</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">CONTROL</th><td>gives final release authority, if the JTAC delegates it; the JTAC always keeps abort authority</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>So: "Bengal 61 has stack and mark, Titus maintains brief and control", or everything at once: "your brief, stack, mark, and control". <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-57 to V-58')}</a></p>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> only a JTAC or FAC(A) clears you, and the JTAC can always abort.</p>

 <h2 id="control">Type of control and method of attack</h2>
 <p>The <Term>game plan</Term> answers two separate questions before the brief. <strong>The type of control</strong> is how the controller will reduce the risk of each attack. <strong>The method of attack</strong> is whether the aircraft must see the target. Any type goes with either method. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-42 to III-43, III-47')}</a></p>
 <h3>Type of control</h3>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Types of terminal attack control" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>Types of terminal attack control <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-2, III-43 to III-47')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">The controller</th><th scope="col">Attacks cleared</th><th scope="col">Clearance</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>{CONTROLS.map((c) => <tr><th scope="row">Type {c.id}</th><td>{c.needs}</td><td>{c.attacks}</td><td>{c.clearance}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
 </table></div>
 <ul>
  <li><strong><Term>Type 1 control</Term></strong>: the controller watches each aircraft and predicts the weapon's path from its geometry, withholding clearance until the aircraft has finished maneuvering, and may restrict weapons that can fly well off the nose. A GPS- or INS-guided weapon flies to its coordinates whatever the geometry, so such weapons should not be controlled under Type 1. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-43')}</a></li>
  <li><strong><Term>Type 2 control</Term></strong>: the controller still controls each attack but cannot see the aircraft at release, or cannot see the target and uses accurate real-time targeting data from another asset. Examples: troops in contact, night, bad weather, high-altitude or standoff weapons. The controller should still try to watch the aircraft. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-44 to III-45')}</a></li>
  <li><strong><Term>Type 3 control</Term></strong>: multiple attacks within one engagement, inside restrictions such as a time, a boundary, headings or a target set. After the readback the controller says <Term>CLEARED TO ENGAGE</Term>; the aircrew calls "COMMENCING ENGAGEMENT" before its first release and "ENGAGEMENT COMPLETE" at the end. The controller monitors and keeps abort authority. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-46')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 <p><strong>No type is "safer".</strong> The type is chosen from the commander's tactical risk assessment, and no level of risk is attached to it: GPS and digital targeting under Type 2 may reduce risk more than Type 1, and none of the three is ordnance-specific. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-42 to III-43')}</a> Two types can be in effect at once for different flights. A change of type must be made before the IN call (or COMMENCING ENGAGEMENT under Type 3); after it, the controller aborts and rebriefs. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-43, III-50')}</a></p>
 <h3>Method of attack</h3>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Bomb on target and bomb on coordinate compared" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>BOT and BOC compared <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-47 to III-49')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col"><Term>BOT</Term> (bomb on target)</th><th scope="col"><Term>BOC</Term> (bomb on coordinate)</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">Must the aircrew see the target?</th><td>Yes: <Term>TALLY</Term>, <Term>CONTACT</Term> or <Term>CAPTURED</Term> on the target or aimpoint</td><td>No, never required to see the target or the mark</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">The coordinates</th><td>Only cue the aircrew and support fires approval</td><td>Must be precise enough for the effect and for fires approval; not changed once read back</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Who reads back</th><td>The lead</td><td>Every aircraft delivering ordnance, from its weapon or system</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Examples</th><td>An LGB lased by the delivering aircraft; unguided bombs under the weather with the aircrew TALLY; an attack corrected from a mark; rockets and guns on a positively identified target</td><td>An inertially aided weapon on a fixed coordinate; an LGB guided by someone outside the flight; unguided bombs from medium or high altitude above an overcast</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>Mixing the two up causes confusion, slows the attack and can lead to fratricide. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-48')}</a></p>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> type of control is about how the controller manages risk; BOT or BOC is about whether you must see the target. They are independent.</p>

 <h2 id="clearance">Clearance calls</h2>
 <p>Weapons release authority gives the controller these calls, and only these: <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-40 to III-41')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Clearance calls" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>Clearance calls <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-40 to III-41')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Call</th><th scope="col">Means</th><th scope="col">Used in</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row"><Term>CLEARED HOT</Term></th><td>Release on this pass. "Ragin' 41, flight, CLEARED HOT" clears the whole flight.</td><td>Types 1 and 2</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row"><Term>CLEARED TO ENGAGE</Term></th><td>Attack within the controller's parameters</td><td>Type 3</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row"><Term>CONTINUE</Term></th><td>Continue the attack profile; release is not yet granted</td><td>All</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row"><Term>CONTINUE DRY</Term></th><td>Continue the pass without releasing, as in training or a show of force ("Type 3, CONTINUE DRY" under Type 3)</td><td>All</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row"><Term>ABORT</Term></th><td>Cease the attack before weapons release</td><td>All</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> the word CLEARED is used only when ordnance is actually to come off, so a bomb never leaves on a dry pass; nonstandard calls are always avoided. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-40')}</a></p>

 <section class="lesson quiz-lesson" data-lesson="control" aria-labelledby="control-title">
  <h3 id="control-title">Quiz: type of control, method and clearance</h3>
  <p class="task" data-prompt aria-live="polite"></p>
  <div class="buttons" data-choices></div>
  <p class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
  <div class="buttons"><button type="button" data-new>Next question</button></div>
 </section>

 <h2 id="checkin">Check-in and game plan</h2>
 <p>The controller says when it wants the <Term>CAS check-in</Term> ("Latch 65 send your check-in"), and may delay it during an attack in progress or ask only for parts. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-15 to V-17')}</a> It comes in this order, remembered as MNPOPCA:</p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="The CAS check-in" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>The CAS check-in <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-15 to V-17')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Item</th><th scope="col">Notes and example</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">Mission number</th><td>From the air tasking order</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Number and type</th><td>"2 by F/A-18C"</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Position and altitude</th><td>"Currently 20 nm north of Ford, block 20-21"</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Ordnance</th><td>With laser codes for LGBs, the Hellfire model and fuzing options</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row"><Term>PLAYTIME</Term></th><td>Or time on station</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Capabilities</th><td>FAC(A), sensors, data link, video downlink code, situation updates and reference graphics on board</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row"><Term>Abort code</Term></th><td>Not needed with secure radios, where ABORT, ABORT, ABORT is standard; a code passed is read back by the controller</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>Aircraft on the air tasking order may check in "as fragged" and keep it short. A controller unsure of an aircraft's capabilities should ask in plain language now, rather than issue instructions it cannot support. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-15, V-17')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>The situation update</strong> gives the aircrew what the mission needs: the surface-to-air threat, the enemy picture, where the friendlies are in general terms ("all friendlies are east of the 94 easting", not friendly grids), artillery that may be a factor, who has clearance authority, the ordnance wanted, and hazards or the controller's laser and IR capability. A long update rattled off to an arriving aircrew will probably not be copied, so it comes in manageable pieces; on check-in, the aircrew says whether it already has the latest picture. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-17 to V-20')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>The game plan</strong> gives at least the type of control and the method of attack, and may add the commander's intent, ordnance and fuzing, effects, the interval and how many grid digits to expect. Questions about capabilities or tactics are settled in plain language before the brief. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-20 to V-21')}</a></p>
 <ul class="exchange">
  <li>"Deuce 21, Type 1, bomb on target; advise when ready for 9-line."</li>
  <li>"Latch 65, Type 2, bomb on coordinate, one GBU-38 instantaneous fusing each aircraft, simultaneous impact, 8-digit grid to follow; advise when ready for 9-line." <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-20 to V-21')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 <p><strong>Simultaneous or sequential.</strong> Simultaneous impacts limit exposure and the enemy's time to react but leave no chance to correct or abort between them. Rules of thumb for a sequential interval: 30 seconds for Type 1 control of unguided ordnance, about a minute for LGBs from medium altitude, more than two minutes for precision weapons from medium to high altitude. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-4 to V-5')}</a></p>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> check in short and in order, and get the game plan settled before anyone reads a 9-line.</p>

 <h2 id="nineline">The 9-line</h2>
 <p>The <Term>9-line</Term> is the standard brief for fixed- and rotary-wing aircraft, in every threat condition, and it does not dictate tactics. Line numbers are not transmitted, units are standard unless briefed, and lines are never passed piecemeal or out of order. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-24 to V-26')}</a> It comes at a moderate pace in three transmissions of three lines, because a brief read too fast gets repeated; if lines 1 to 3 are left out, the next transmission starts with "Elevation". <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-26')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="The 9-line brief" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>The 9-line CAS brief and the example in Figure 2 <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-25 to V-32')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Line</th><th scope="col">Item</th><th scope="col">Given as</th><th scope="col">Example</th><th scope="col">Read back</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>{NINE_LINE.map((l) => <tr><th scope="row">{l.line ?? '—'}</th><td>{l.title}</td><td>{l.unit}</td><td>{l.says}</td><td>{l.readback ? 'Yes' : ''}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
 </table></div>

 <section class="lesson figure-lesson" data-lesson="brief" aria-labelledby="brief-title">
  <h3 id="brief-title" class="fig-title">Figure 2 — A 9-line on the map</h3>
  <p>Step through the brief from the opening example. Each line lights up what it puts on the map; the last button shows the aircrew's readback. Game plan: "{EXAMPLE.aircraft}, {EXAMPLE.gamePlan}; advise when ready for 9-line."</p>
  <div class="buttons chips" data-steps></div>
  <div class="fig-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Figure 2, scrolls sideways on narrow screens" tabindex="0"><svg class="natops" viewBox="0 0 640 340" role="img" aria-label="Plan view of the example brief: the IP east of the target, the run-in line heading 270, the offset side, the final attack heading window, the friendlies south and the egress north"></svg></div>
  <p class="no-js">Interactive figure unavailable. The example: IP Hammer, heading 270 offset right, 8.2 NM; 450 feet, two armored vehicles in a tree line, NV 1234 5678; smoke, south 800, egress north to Anvil block 14 to 16; final attack headings 240 to 300, TOT 1430. Readback: 450 feet, NV 1234 5678, final attack headings 240 to 300, TOT 1430.</p>
  <div class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <p class="fig-caption">An invented example in the format of JP 3-09.3 Figure V-9: the call signs, places and numbers are made up. Drawn to scale, north up, with the IP on the reciprocal of line 2 and the friendlies placed from line 8. The attack-heading window is the aircraft headings allowed into the target; the run-in from the IP lies inside it.</p>
 </section>

 <details class="more"><summary>More on each line</summary>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Line 1, <Term id="Initial point">IP</Term> or <Term id="Battle position">BP</Term>.</strong> The IP is where the run-in starts; for helicopters the BP is where attacks are commenced, and a hasty BP is given by its center grid. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-26')}</a> When attacking from an orbit over the target, lines 1 to 3 can be "from the overhead". <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-75')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Line 2, heading and offset.</strong> Degrees magnetic from the IP (or BP center) to the target, as three digits: "one eight zero". The <Term>offset direction</Term> is given only if restricted: the side of the IP-to-target line the aircrew may maneuver on, from the IP until release or egress. It regulates the attack quadrant without assigning a heading and keeps aircraft clear of a <Term>GTL</Term> or a threat. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-26, III-75 to III-76')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Line 3, distance.</strong> IP to target in nautical miles to a tenth for fixed-wing; BP center to target in meters, to the nearest 100, for attack helicopters. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-26')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Line 4, elevation.</strong> Feet MSL, read as digits with "feet" after to separate it from line 5; any other datum, such as height above ellipsoid, must be said. Elevation is the hardest line to get right, and steeper impact angles reduce the effect of an error in it. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-1, V-26 to V-27')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Line 5, description</strong>, specific enough to recognize: "Five APCs in the open". If the commander wants the people inside a building, line 5 says so ("HVT in 2-story building") rather than describing the building. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-2, V-27')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Line 6, location.</strong> A grid including the 100,000-meter square, a latitude and longitude with the format made clear, or an offset from a known point. The number of digits is the grid's precision, not its accuracy. For an area target, the center or greatest concentration; for a moving target, a point on its route with direction and speed. The datum must be known, and is confirmed if not WGS 84. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-27 to V-28')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Line 7, mark or terminal guidance</strong>: smoke, laser or IR, with the laser code; or, when someone else lases for the weapon, their call sign, "laser" and the code: "Blackjack laser, code 1688". A BOC attack with no mark says "no mark". <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-5, V-30')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Line 8, friendlies</strong>: cardinal or sub-cardinal direction and meters from the target to the closest friendly position, "South 300". The controller may not be the closest friendly. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-3, V-30')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Line 9, egress</strong>, starting with "egress", by direction and control point, with the altitude in numbers rather than "in your block": "Egress North to Moon, block 15-17". <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-30')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Remarks, then restrictions</strong>, only those not already briefed. Remarks first: the <Term>laser target line</Term> (always passed when a ground designator is used), the ordnance or effects wanted, the surface-to-air threat and suppression plan, other fires, calls requested such as IP inbound or <Term>IN</Term> with heading, and hazards, weather and the friendly mark. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-30 to V-31')}</a> Then the restrictions, all read back: <Term>danger close</Term> with the commander's initials, airspace coordination areas, <Term id="Final attack heading">final attack headings</Term>, lateral or altitude separation, approval out of the BP for helicopters, and the <Term>TOT</Term> or time to target, last, to mark the end of the brief. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-31 to V-32')}</a> Controllers choose the least restrictive headings that meet the commander's intent, because tight ones slow the attack, hurt survivability and leave aircraft outside parameters. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-32')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 </details>

 <section class="lesson quiz-lesson" data-lesson="lines" aria-labelledby="lines-title">
  <h3 id="lines-title">Quiz: which line is that?</h3>
  <p class="task" data-prompt aria-live="polite"></p>
  <div class="buttons" data-choices></div>
  <p class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
  <div class="buttons"><button type="button" data-new>Next line</button></div>
 </section>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> three transmissions of three lines, then remarks and restrictions, with the TOT last.</p>

 <h2 id="readback">Readbacks</h2>
 <p>The <Term>mandatory readback</Term> is <strong>line 4, line 6 and every restriction</strong>. Final attack headings, airspace coordination areas, danger close and times on target are always restrictions and always read back; so is anything else the controller asks for. Offsets are restrictions but read back only if requested. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-33 to V-34')}</a> In a <Term>BOT</Term> attack the lead reads back; in a <Term>BOC</Term> attack every aircraft delivering ordnance reads back lines 4 and 6 from its weapon or system. An aircraft with a known bad navigation system says so rather than read back from it. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-33')}</a></p>
 <ul class="exchange">
  <li><strong>Correct:</strong> "Call sign, readback correct" or "good readback".</li>
  <li><strong>Wrong:</strong> the controller restates the part in question, stressing what was wrong: "Deuce 21, be advised, final attack headings 1-8-0 through 2-1-0". <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-33')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 <p>NATO controllers, following Allied Tactical Publication 3.3.2.1, pass the line titles before each item and also require line 8 to be read back. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-26, V-30')}</a> General readback technique is in <a href={radio}>Radio Procedures</a>.</p>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> elevation, location, restrictions. Lead only for BOT; every shooter, from the system, for BOC.</p>

 <h2 id="correlation">Correlation</h2>
 <p><Term>Correlation</Term> means the controller confirming that the aircrew has the correct target or the correct target data, and it is required on each and every CAS attack. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-34')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>BOC:</strong> complete when the aircraft correctly reads back lines 4 and 6 from its system, and the restrictions.</li>
  <li><strong>BOT:</strong> the controller marks the target, positions the aircraft to see it and asks questions with unique answers until satisfied, then says what the target is: "The third vehicle is your target", never just "That's your target". The aircrew answers <Term>TALLY</Term>, <Term>CAPTURED</Term> or <Term>CONTACT</Term>. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-34')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 <p>A visual mark usually lands 30 to 45 seconds before the TOT. The aircrew calls "CONTACT the mark", and the controller corrects from it in direction and meters: "Razor 53, from the mark, east 50". Anything else used for cueing is not called "the mark" but something like "lead's hits". <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-40')}</a> Correlation is also needed with any <Term>third party contributor</Term>, such as a remote observer or another aircraft lasing. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-5')}</a> The marks themselves, and the laser and IR call sequences, are in <a href="#marks">Marks, laser and infrared calls</a>; describing a target with no mark is the <a href="#talkon">talk-on</a>.</p>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> correlate on every attack: by seeing the target or mark in BOT, by a correct system readback in BOC.</p>

 <h2 id="attack">IN, cleared hot, abort and reattack</h2>
 <p>On the run-in the aircrew makes the calls requested, such as "IP inbound", then <Term>IN</Term> with the heading or direction if one was restricted, early enough to be cleared before the release window: "IN heading 360". <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-44 to III-45')}</a></p>
 <p>Once an aircraft calls IN, everyone else holds their calls until it is cleared or aborted, except that anyone can and should call an abort at any time: the tactical air direction net belongs to the controller. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-46')}</a> After release the aircrew reports <Term>OFF</Term> and what it released: "Faction 31, off, one away, 27 seconds." <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-54')}</a> The common brevity words cover the shot itself: <Term>GUNS</Term> for the gun, <Term>RIFLE</Term> for an air-to-surface missile launch and <Term>MAGNUM</Term> for an anti-radiation missile.</p>
 <p>For Types 1 and 2 the controller answers IN with <Term>CLEARED HOT</Term>, <Term>CONTINUE DRY</Term> or <Term>ABORT</Term>, and under Type 1 must see the aircraft and judge its geometry first. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-44 to III-45')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Abort.</strong> The controller shall abort an aircraft not aligned with the correct target, and must abort it if friendly troops may be endangered or for the aircrew's safety. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-47')}</a></p>
 <ul class="exchange">
  <li><strong>With an <Term>abort code</Term>:</strong> "SPIKE 41, NAIL 11, ABORT DELTA, ABORT DELTA, ABORT DELTA"</li>
  <li><strong>Without:</strong> "ABORT, ABORT, ABORT" <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-47')}</a></li>
 </ul>
 <p>A <Term>PLA</Term>, steering a guided weapon away after release, is a last resort: only some weapons can do it, the commander must approve it and its impact point before launch, and silence is not consent. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-41 to III-42')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Reattack and assessment.</strong> The controller judges whether the commander's effects were achieved. Under Types 1 and 2 a reattack is requested and cleared again; under Type 3 the aircraft may reattack while its CLEARED TO ENGAGE stands. Corrections come in direction and meters from the last impact: "Razor 02, from lead's hits, north 100"; for the same target, "CONTINUE for a reattack, restrictions remain the same." <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-48')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Battle damage assessment</strong> is passed for the flight, not each aircraft, as they egress, or at least SUCCESSFUL, UNSUCCESSFUL or UNKNOWN. "If you do not see it, do not report it." <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-49')}</a></p>
 <p><strong>Threats.</strong> Against a SAM launch the pilot breaks and dispenses countermeasures; against AAA, jinks. The controller calls it at once with the location from the target: "Latch 01, SAM launch southwest of the target." Aircraft shot down in CAS were usually hit by threats they did not know about. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-51 to III-52')}</a></p>
 <p class="key"><strong>Key point:</strong> after an IN call, everyone else holds their calls until that aircraft is cleared or aborted, except that anyone may call abort.</p>

 <h2 id="recap">Key points</h2>
 <ul class="recap">
  <li>Only a qualified JTAC or FAC(A) clears an attack; the JTAC keeps abort authority.</li>
  <li>Type of control (how risk is managed) and method of attack (BOT or BOC) are separate choices in the game plan.</li>
  <li>Check-in in MNPOPCA order; 9-line in three groups of three, then remarks, restrictions and the TOT last.</li>
  <li>Read back elevation, location and every restriction.</li>
  <li>Correlate on every attack. Under Types 1 and 2 each attack is IN, CLEARED HOT (or abort), <Term>OFF</Term>; under Type 3 one CLEARED TO ENGAGE covers the engagement, bracketed by COMMENCING ENGAGEMENT and ENGAGEMENT COMPLETE.</li>
  <li>CLEARED means ordnance comes off; anyone can call ABORT.</li>
 </ul>

 <h2 id="marks">Marks, laser and infrared calls</h2>
 <p>A mark builds awareness, picks out a target in an array, reduces the chance of fratricide and supports control. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-7')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Types of mark" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>Types of mark <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-6 to V-10')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Mark</th><th scope="col">How it works</th><th scope="col">Watch for</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">Laser handoff</th><td>A designator's energy for the aircraft's laser spot tracker, day or night; high confidence with the right geometry</td><td>Needs headings that keep the tracker from locking onto the designator itself</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Infrared sparkle</th><td>Matching the aircraft's IR pointer to the controller's, or walking the aircraft's sparkle onto the target by voice</td><td>Night only; the pointer may give the controller away to an enemy with night vision</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Video downlink talk-on</th><td>The controller sees the aircraft's sensor picture</td><td></td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Offset from a <Term>target reference point</Term></th><td>Or from a grid reference the aircrew knows</td><td></td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Indirect fire</th><td>Artillery or mortar smoke; most effective within 100 m of the target, generally effective enough within 300 m <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-78')}</a></td><td>Usually needs a correction and sacrifices surprise</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Direct fire, data link, marks of opportunity</th><td>Tracers or grenades; data link tracks; burning vehicles and the like</td><td></td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <h3>Laser calls</h3>
 <p>For a laser mark the aircrew gives a ten-second warning. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-79')}</a> A laser handoff runs: <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-35 to V-36')}</a></p>
 <ol class="exchange">
  <li><span class="who">Controller</span> <span class="said">Sends the aircraft inbound on a heading with a <Term>STARE</Term> on the code and the point.</span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Aircrew</span> <span class="said"><Term>TEN SECONDS</Term>, then <Term>LASER ON</Term>.</span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Controller</span> <span class="said"><Term>LASING</Term> and the code.</span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Aircrew</span> <span class="said"><Term>SPOT</Term> when it has it, and <Term>CEASE LASER</Term>.</span></li>
  <li><span class="who">Controller</span> <span class="said">Asks what is under the crosshairs and confirms the target.</span></li>
 </ol>
 <p><Term>SPOT</Term> is not <Term>CAPTURED</Term>: spot jitter or spillover can put the spot slightly off the target, which is why the controller confirms. On <Term>NEGATIVE LASER</Term>, the controller checks the designator, the code and the aircraft's position, and tries again or picks another mark. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-35 to V-36')}</a></p>
 <h3>Infrared calls</h3>
 <p>When the ground marks with an IR pointer, the aircrew must call <Term>VISUAL</Term> for the friendly position and <Term>TALLY</Term> or <Term>CONTACT SPARKLE</Term> on every pass before it is cleared: proof it can tell the friendly end of the beam from the target end. The controller may <Term>SNAKE</Term> the beam, then <Term>STEADY</Term> it, <Term>CEASE SPARKLE</Term>, or ask the aircraft to <Term>MATCH SPARKLE</Term>. <Term>ROPE</Term>, circling the beam around the aircraft to show the friendly position, can damage night vision devices and is not recommended for helicopters. IR marks start 20 to 30 seconds before the TOT. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-30, III-80')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="talkon">Talk-on technique</h2>
 <p>With no mark, or to finish one, the controller describes the target onto the aircrew's eyes or sensors with a <Term>talk-on</Term>: <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-40 to V-44')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Perspective.</strong> Vertical relief stands out from the ground and a low helicopter, but a fixed-wing aircraft overhead sees horizontal relief better; beyond about 15,000 ft slant range colors cannot be told apart by eye.</li>
  <li><strong>Big to small</strong> for a visual talk-on, from a feature both can identify; a sensor talk-on in a city may start at a specific intersection.</li>
  <li><strong><Term>FIDO</Term></strong> for each step: from a point, in a direction, a distance, to an object, then ask for CONTACT: "Razor 53, from the cubby-hole, proceed south across MSR Michigan to the first building and call CONTACT."</li>
  <li><strong>Keep it simple.</strong> No more than two cardinal directions in a transmission; name features as you go ("that building will be called the bank"); set a unit of measure.</li>
  <li><strong>Two to one.</strong> Two directive statements, then a confirming question.</li>
  <li><strong>Finish</strong> with questions only the right target can answer: "Which direction is the lead vehicle facing?"</li>
 </ul>
 <p>For a low-level attack with only seconds to find the target, the controller may give an <strong>enhanced target description</strong> of what the aircrew will see along the attack heading; the aircrew then does not have to call CONTACT. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-42 to V-43')}</a></p>

 <h2 id="fratricide">Preventing fratricide</h2>
 <p>Friendly fire has occasionally come from malfunctioning weapons, but more often from confusion: misidentified targets, wrong locations or descriptions, locations sent or copied wrongly, and lost awareness anywhere in the chain. Everyone must try to identify friendly units, the enemy and civilians before targeting, clearing fires and releasing. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-10')}</a></p>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Clearance of fires.</strong> Release authority belongs to the supported commander, who delegates it to the controller for specific engagements; attacks are cleared only with the standard calls, and CLEARED only when ordnance is to come off. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-40')}</a></li>
  <li><strong><Term>Danger close</Term>.</strong> <Term id="Risk-estimate distance">Risk-estimate distances</Term> are for a 0.1 percent (1 in 1,000) probability of incapacitation; delivery inside that distance is danger close. The supported commander accepts the risk by passing their initials, through the controller, to the aircraft, and it is a restriction that is read back. When ordnance is a factor in friendly safety, the axis of attack should be parallel to the friendly force's axis, so a long or short hit does not land on them. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-36 to III-37, V-32')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Attack headings.</strong> As nearly parallel to the <Term>FLOT</Term> as possible, because hits fall long or short along the bomb-fall line, and not planned over occupied friendly positions. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-11')}</a></li>
  <li><strong><Term>Troops in contact</Term></strong> means friendly forces receiving effective fire. It signals urgency; it does not dictate a type of control or remove anyone's responsibility to avoid friendly and civilian casualties. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-36')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Friendly locations.</strong> Never used as the target location in a request: fratricide has happened when controllers used friendly locations as routing points. Friendly grids are avoided in the situation update. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-3, V-18')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Friendly marking.</strong> IR position markers (visible only through night vision devices, best flashing) and GLINT tape, confirmed by voice with the aircrew. With an IR pointer as the mark, the aircrew must be VISUAL and TALLY or CONTACT SPARKLE before clearance. Marking friendlies to help find the target is the least desirable method, used only when nothing else works. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-30, III-81')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Without a JTAC.</strong> The aircrew carries more of the integration: it gets the target elevation, location, description, marks, nearest friendlies and restrictions from the ground unit, sets its own restrictions if needed, correlates by talking with people not trained in it, and tells them to say "abort" if anything becomes unsafe. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-54 to V-55')}</a></li>
 </ul>

 <h2 id="airframes">Fixed- and rotary-wing CAS across the services</h2>
 <p>Every service organizes, trains and equips for CAS within its roles. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-2')}</a> Fighter and attack aircraft usually fly in groups of two to four: Air Force <Term id="Flight">flights</Term>, Navy and Marine Corps <Term id="Section">sections</Term> (two) or divisions (three to four). Bombers usually fly singly or as two-ships. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-4 to I-5')}</a></p>
 <h3>Fixed-wing</h3>
 <p>Speed and range make fixed-wing aircraft flexible, and they carry the full range of unguided, precision, area-denial and forward-firing weapons. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-5')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Fixed-wing CAS altitudes compared" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>Fixed-wing tactics by height</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Gains</th><th scope="col">Costs</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">Medium and high, above about 8,000 ft AGL <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-84 to III-85')}</a></th><td>Target, marks and others' hits stay in view; saves fuel; clear of AAA and shoulder-launched SAMs</td><td>Needs local air superiority and good weather; harder for the controller to see; degrades unguided accuracy</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Low and very low <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-86 to III-88')}</a></th><td>Denies long-range detection; works under low weather</td><td>Harder navigation and target acquisition; exposed to small arms and AAA. The run-in from the IP is the most crucial phase.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <p>At low altitude the controller marks 30 to 45 seconds before the TOT and gives final corrections from the mark. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-88')}</a> The deliveries themselves, level, loft, pop-up and dive, are in <a href={wd}>Weapons Delivery</a>.</p>
 <h3>Rotary-wing</h3>
 <p>Attack helicopters reposition firepower quickly, carry forward- and off-axis-firing weapons, loiter, fly nap of the earth and work in varied terrain. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-5')}</a> They move from a holding area to a <Term>battle position</Term>, pick individual firing points and stay masked until the time or order to attack. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-93')}</a> They can often work under ceilings that stop fixed-wing CAS, while fixed-wing aircraft can work above blowing dust that grounds helicopters. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-8')}</a> Off-axis weapons mean a helicopter need not point at the target, so a controller may give a munition attack azimuth instead of an aircraft heading. <a href="#source-jp">{j('V-11')}</a></p>
 <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Helicopter firing modes" tabindex="0"><table>
  <caption>Helicopter firing modes <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-93 to III-94')}</a></caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Mode</th><th scope="col">Best for</th><th scope="col">Trade-off</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
   <tr><th scope="row">Hovering fire</th><td>Precision weapons</td><td>Only briefly, and from cover</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Running fire</th><td>Rockets and guns, steadier in level forward flight</td><td>A harder target than a hover</td></tr>
   <tr><th scope="row">Diving fire</th><td>Unguided weapons; may be the most accurate, often from an overhead pattern</td><td>Leaves the aircraft exposed</td></tr>
  </tbody>
 </table></div>
 <h3>By service</h3>
 <ul>
  <li><strong>Marine Corps</strong> attack helicopters are part of the Marine air-ground task force, trained and equipped for CAS, usually in mixed sections of attack and utility helicopters. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-5')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Army</strong> attack helicopters fight as maneuver units and use <Term>close combat attack</Term> procedures. CCA is not synonymous with CAS, and the Army does not consider its attack helicopters a CAS system, so a controller should not expect Army aircrew to use CAS procedures without further coordination. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-5, III-89')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Special operations</strong> fixed- and rotary-wing aircraft use CAS, CCA, gunship and FAC(A) procedures. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-5')}</a> A <strong>joint air attack team</strong> combines fixed-wing CAS aircraft and helicopters against high-priority targets. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-5 to I-6')}</a></li>
  <li><strong>Unmanned aircraft</strong> can find and mark targets, talk other aircraft on, lase, or attack using CAS procedures. <a href="#source-jp">{j('I-6')}</a> <strong>Bombers</strong> may stay on station eight hours or more and check the target with their own sensors from as far as 40 nautical miles. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-97')}</a></li>
 </ul>

 <h2 id="fiveline">The rotary-wing 5-line</h2>
 <p>The <Term>5-line</Term> is a "friendly-centric" brief that quickly orients helicopters to a target from the friendly position. Its format matches the Army's CCA brief and the special operations call for fire, but it is still a CAS brief: passing it is not clearance to fire, so the type of control and method of attack come first. It assumes the aircrew can find the friendlies; if not, for example on an unfamiliar battlefield with no clear front line, the controller uses the 9-line. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-94 to III-95')}</a></p>
 <ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Observer, warning order and game plan</strong>: "5-line, Type 2 control, BOT, rockets and guns".</li>
  <li><strong>Friendly location and mark</strong>: a named point rather than a friendly grid, and how it is marked, such as an IR strobe.</li>
  <li><strong>Target location</strong>: magnetic bearing and range in meters from the friendlies, a reference point or a grid; an offset from a known point suits an aircrew looking out better than coordinates.</li>
  <li><strong>Target description and mark</strong>: type, number, orientation and protection, and how it is marked.</li>
  <li><strong>Remarks and restrictions</strong>, all read back: attack headings, the threat and suppression, airspace coordination areas, danger close and initials, and timing.</li>
 </ol>
 <p>It is passed as one transmission, with long restrictions as a second. The helicopters push as soon as they have read back, so a TOT must be made clear in the restrictions; with Army or special operations helicopters, which may not know the 5-line's restrictions, the controller adds "At my command" to keep control of the timing. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-95 to III-96')}</a> The publication's example:</p>
 <ul class="exchange">
  <li><strong>JTAC:</strong> "Deuce 31, Broadsword 11, 5-line, Type 2 control, BOT, rockets and guns, my position is checkpoint 295 marked by IR strobe, northwest 200, single technical vehicle marked by IR SPARKLE, make all attacks over my right shoulder, left pull, keep all effects of fires west of MSR Clovis."</li>
  <li><strong>Aircraft:</strong> "Deuce 31 copies over your right shoulder, left pull, keep all effects of fires west of MSR Clovis, pushing."</li>
  <li><strong>JTAC:</strong> "Deuce 31, CONTINUE."</li>
  <li><strong>Aircraft:</strong> "Deuce 31 and flight, VISUAL, TALLY, IN."</li>
  <li><strong>JTAC:</strong> "Deuce 31 and flight, CLEARED HOT." <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-97')}</a></li>
 </ul>

 <section class="sim-panel" aria-labelledby="sims">
  <h2 id="sims">In the sim</h2>
  <p>DCS World's AI JTAC follows the same sequence as the real thing, simplified to radio-menu calls. This is how the <Term record="a-10">A-10C II</Term> manual describes it; other DCS aircraft and Falcon BMS were not checked for this revision.</p>
  <h3>The flow in DCS</h3>
  <ol class="steps">
   <li>Open the radio menu, pick <strong>JTACs</strong> (F4) and the JTAC, on the frequency in the briefing unless Easy Communications tunes it for you, and <strong>check in</strong> with your playtime. The automatic check-in sends your mission number, position from the IP and altitude, weapons and playtime. <a href="#source-a10">{'[2, pp. 784–785]'}</a></li>
   <li>The JTAC replies with the <strong>type of control</strong> and asks if you are ready for the 9-line. Choose <strong>Ready to copy</strong>; it reads the nine lines, then the remarks when you are ready (weapon, weather or attack headings). <a href="#source-a10">{'[2, p. 785]'}</a></li>
   <li><strong>Read back</strong> the target location and elevation, and anything else applicable, such as the final attack heading. The A-10C II then gets a digital 9-line on its MSG page and a red target triangle on the TAD. <a href="#source-a10">{'[2, p. 785]'}</a></li>
   <li>What happens next depends on the mark: <a href="#source-a10">{'[2, pp. 786–787]'}</a>
    <ul>
     <li><strong>Coordinate only</strong>: the JTAC clears you to engage; call <strong>Attack Complete</strong> when done.</li>
     <li><strong>Smoke</strong>: call <strong>IP Inbound</strong>; white smoke goes down within 10 NM and the JTAC says "mark is on the deck"; call <strong>Contact the Mark</strong> and take the correction from it; call <strong>In</strong>, get cleared hot or aborted, and call <strong>Off</strong>.</li>
     <li><strong>Laser</strong> (default code 1688): IP Inbound, <strong>Laser On</strong>, find it with the spot tracker and call <strong>Spot</strong>, then In and Off as above.</li>
     <li><strong>IR pointer</strong>: as smoke, seen through night vision goggles, with <strong>Pulse</strong> and <strong>Rope</strong> options.</li>
    </ul>
   </li>
   <li>Other calls: Repeat Brief, What is my target?, <strong>Contact</strong> (you report what is at your sensor point and the JTAC confirms or corrects), Request BDA, Unable to comply and Check Out. <a href="#source-a10">{'[2, pp. 787–788]'}</a></li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Where it differs from the joint publication</h3>
  <ul>
   <li>The check-in leaves out capabilities and the abort code, and the JTAC's reply gives the type of control but the manual does not mention a method of attack. <a href="#source-a10">{'[2, p. 785]'}</a></li>
   <li>The manual calls Type 1 "the most common and restrictive" type, "most often used when friendly forces are 'danger close'". <a href="#source-a10">{'[2, p. 784]'}</a> The joint publication attaches no level of risk to a type of control. <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-43')}</a></li>
   <li>The laser sequence is shortened to Laser On and Spot, with no TEN SECONDS, LASING or CEASE LASER. A coordinate attack ends with "Attack Complete" rather than ENGAGEMENT COMPLETE. <a href="#source-a10">{'[2, pp. 786–787]'}</a> <a href="#source-jp">{j('III-46, V-35 to V-36')}</a></li>
   <li>Not described in the manual, so not claimed either way: FAC(A) duty splits, danger close initials, abort codes and the helicopter 5-line.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>To practise:</strong> fly the sequence in this module's order, read back from your system before the JTAC asks, and expect DCS to accept the shortened calls above.</p>
 </section>

 <h2 id="terms">Terms</h2>
 <p>Every CAS word is in the <a href={glossary}>close air support terms</a>; brevity words such as TALLY and CLEARED HOT are in the <a href={sitePath('encyclopedia/terminology/brevity/')}>brevity words</a>.</p>
 <GlossaryList items={[
  'Terminal attack control', 'Terminal guidance operations', 'Type 1 control', 'Type 2 control', 'Type 3 control', 'BOT', 'BOC', 'Correlation',
  'CAS check-in', 'Game plan', '9-line', '5-line', 'Initial point', 'Battle position', 'Offset direction', 'Final attack heading',
  'Laser target line', 'Mandatory readback', 'Talk-on', 'FIDO', { id: 'Danger close', set: 'Close air support' }, 'Risk-estimate distance', 'Troops in contact',
  'Close combat attack', 'CONTACT', 'CAPTURED', 'SPOT', 'CONTACT SPARKLE', 'CLEARED TO ENGAGE', 'IN', 'OFF', 'GUNS', 'RIFLE',
 ]} />
